@startuml
title Period-Based TMR4 Step Engine — Single ISR, Bresenham + Velocity Inside ISR
' Current architecture as of March 2026 (scurve_motion branch)
' GRBL-exact three-pass planner runs in main loop after each enqueue.
' ISR is TIMER_4_InterruptHandler (TMR4 overflow, IPL6 shadow regs).
' ISR is pure integer: reads pre-computed rate fields, no float, no division.
' OC1 hardware (dual-compare) auto-toggles step GPIO — no OC1 interrupt used.

scale 1.5

participant "TMR4\n(Period-Based\n781.25 kHz)" as TMR4
participant "TIMER_4_ISR\n(IPL6 Shadow Regs\nBresenham + Velocity)" as ISR
participant "TMR5\n(One-Shot\nPulse Width)" as TMR5
participant "Step GPIO\n(Harmony Macros)" as GPIO

== Segment Load (main loop — MOTION_Tasks / STEPPER_LoadSegment) ==
note over TMR4, GPIO
  Runs once per segment in main loop BEFORE first step fires:
    delta[], abs_delta[], dominant_axis, direction_bits, error[] loaded into ISR statics
    Direction GPIO pins set here (DirX_Set/Clear etc.) — must be stable before first STEP
    PR4 = initial_rate  (TMR4 rollover period = step interval)
    OC1 dual-compare auto-generates step pulse in hardware (no OC1 interrupt)
    TMR4_Start() if not already running
end note

== Per-Step Cycle (repeats every PR4 ticks — TMR4 overflow) ==

TMR4 -> GPIO : OC1 hardware auto-toggles step pin HIGH at compare match\n(silicon — no CPU involvement for pulse rising edge)
TMR4 -> ISR : TMR4 overflows at PR4 → TIMER_4_InterruptHandler fires (IPL6)
activate ISR
note over ISR
  M14K Shadow Register Set (IPL5):
  Entire CPU register file swapped in hardware in 1 cycle.
  Zero push/pop stack cost on ISR entry/exit.
end note

ISR -> ISR : Guard: cache seg = app_data_ref->currentSegment\n             return immediately if NULL

ISR -> GPIO : Dominant axis STEP HIGH\n              switch(dominant_axis) {\n                case AXIS_X: StepX_Set(); break;\n                case AXIS_Y: StepY_Set(); break;\n                case AXIS_Z: StepZ_Set(); break;\n                default:     StepA_Set(); break; }\n              Compiler: jump table + Harmony #define -> single LATxSET write.\n              No function call, no pointer indirection.

ISR -> ISR : Position counter update\n             direction_bits & (1U << dominant_axis)\n             -> AXIS_IncrementSteps / AXIS_DecrementSteps\n             (always_inline — address never in pointer array -> genuinely inlines)

loop Bresenham subordinate axes (max 3 iterations, bounded)
    ISR -> ISR : error[axis] += abs_delta[axis]\n                 abs_delta[] precomputed at segment load — no abs() call in ISR
    alt error[axis] >= dominant_delta (this axis needs a step this cycle)
        ISR -> GPIO : switch(axis) -> StepY_Set() / StepZ_Set() / StepA_Set()\n                      Single LATxSET write, no call overhead
        ISR -> ISR : AXIS_IncrementSteps / AXIS_DecrementSteps
        ISR -> ISR : error[axis] -= dominant_delta
    end
end

note right of ISR
  Bresenham variation: 0-3 subordinate axes may step
  per ISR call. Bounded worst-case — not unbounded.
  Identical pattern to GRBL reference firmware.
end note

ISR -> TMR5 : T5CONSET = _T5CON_ON_MASK\n              Direct SFR — starts one-shot pulse-width timer

ISR -> ISR : Velocity profiling (integer only — no float, no division)\n             sc = seg->steps_completed\n             ACCEL  (sc < accelerate_until): step_interval -= rate_delta\n             DECEL  (sc >= decelerate_after): step_interval += decel_rate_delta\n             CRUISE: step_interval unchanged

ISR -> ISR : Schedule next step — direct SFR writes\n             period = (uint16_t)step_interval  (clamp: min 7 ticks)\n             PR4 = period  (TMR4 rolls over here — sets next ISR interval)

ISR -> ISR : seg->steps_completed++
deactivate ISR

TMR4 -> TMR4 : Rolls over at PR4, resets to 0\n                Next step cycle begins automatically

== Pulse Width Expiry (~2.56 µs after STEP HIGH) ==

TMR5 -> TMR5 : Period expires -> TMR5_PulseWidthCallback()
activate TMR5
TMR5 -> TMR5 : T5CONCLR = _T5CON_ON_MASK  (direct SFR — stop one-shot)
TMR5 -> GPIO : StepX_Clear()\n               StepY_Clear()\n               StepZ_Clear()\n               StepA_Clear()\n               4 unrolled Harmony macros -> 4 LATxCLR writes\n               No loop, no function pointer, no call overhead
note right of GPIO
  All 4 axes cleared unconditionally every cycle.
  DRV8825 min pulse: 1 µs
  TMC5160 min pulse: 100 ns
  2.56 µs satisfies both with margin.
end note
deactivate TMR5

== Segment Completion (main loop — MOTION_Tasks) ==
note over TMR4, GPIO
  Main loop polls: seg->steps_completed >= seg->steps_remaining
  On completion:
    currentSegment = NULL   (ISR guard returns immediately on next fire)
    motionQueueCount--
    motionSegmentCompleted = true  (triggers deferred "ok" check)
  If queue empty: TMR4_Stop(), motionActive = false
  If more segments: STEPPER_LoadSegment() -> motion continues seamlessly
end note

== Design Decisions ==
note over TMR4, GPIO
  GPIO (board-specific, MCC-managed):
    Step/Clear  -> switch + Harmony #define macros (plib_gpio.h)
                   MCC pin renames propagate automatically
    Direction   -> set once per segment in STEPPER_LoadSegment() (main loop)
                   Stepper drivers require DIR stable BEFORE first STEP rising edge

  Timer (silicon — never changes board-to-board):
    PR4               -> direct SFR register (sets TMR4 period / step interval)
    T5CONSET/T5CONCLR -> direct SFR registers (pulse width one-shot)
    OC1 dual-compare  -> auto-generates step pulse GPIO in hardware, no ISR

  Shadow register set:
    All motion state (delta[], abs_delta[], error[], direction_bits,
    dominant_axis, dominant_delta) are module-static variables.
    ISR reads/writes them with zero stack allocation.

  Segment transition race window:
    currentSegment assigned before STEPPER_LoadSegment() populates ISR statics.
    One ISR fire in this window uses prior-segment statics — practically harmless.
    Same pattern as GRBL reference firmware.
end note

@enduml